diagnosis_text:
End-to-end reachability between the reported endpoints is healthy, and there is no evidence of black-holing on the super-spine. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1, topology-wide probes show 0% loss and ~1.3 ms RTT (get_reachability: tx=2, rx=2, status ok). Return path from pc_0_1 to pc_0_0 is likewise clean.

Control-plane and forwarding state across the fabric are consistent:
- leaf_router_0_0 routes 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.8 on eth1 (BGP) and has BGP up with both spines (show ip bgp summary: 2 neighbors, prefixes received).
- leaf_router_0_1 routes 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.10 on eth1 (BGP) with both spine sessions up.
- Both spines have active BGP sessions to their leaves and to the super-spine, with routes to both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 installed (e.g., spine_router_0_0: B> to 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.5 and 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.7).
- On super_spine_router_0, BGP neighbors 172.16.0.1 and 172.16.0.3 are up with two prefixes each, and its RIB prefers 172.16.0.3 for both /24s (B>* 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.3). This reflects a valid best-path choice, not a drop.

Mechanistically, intra-pod traffic between the two leaves is forwarded via the spines directly and does not require the super-spine; all devices have correct next-hops installed. Root cause: No network fault present at this time; the reported outage is not reproducible. Corrective fix: No restart is required—leave super_spine_router_0 and the rest of the fabric as-is and close the ticket; monitor for intermittent host issues if users report recurring problems.
